```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import MapView, { Marker } from 'react-native-maps';
import { Offline } from 'react-native-offline';

const TourismGuide = () => {
  const [isOffline, setIsOffline] = useState(false);

  const handleConnectivityChange = (isConnected) => {
    setIsOffline(isConnected);
  };

  return (
    <Offline onConnectivityChange={handleConnectivityChange}>
      <MapView
        style={{ flex: 1 }}
        initialRegion={{
          latitude: 40.7128,
          longitude: -74.0060,
          latitudeDelta: 0.0922,
          longitudeDelta: 0.0421,
        }}
      >
        {isOffline ? (
          <Marker
            coordinate={{
              latitude: 40.7128,
              longitude: -74.0060,
            }}
            title="Point of Interest"
          />
        ) : (
          <Marker
            coordinate={{
              latitude: 40.7128,
              longitude: -74.0060,
            }}
            title="Point of Interest"
          />
        )}
      </MapView>
    </Offline>
  );
};

export default TourismGuide;
```

In this example, we're using the `Offline` component to detect network connectivity and switch between online and offline modes. When the app is offline, we display a marker on the map. You can customize the `initialRegion` prop to set the initial map view and add more markers for points of interest.

Remember to add the necessary permissions in your `AndroidManifest.xml` and `Info.plist` files for accessing the device's location and displaying the map.
